# docsys — the backup container.
#
# Its own image rather than a job inside the application's, for three reasons,
# and each of them is the kind that only shows up at the worst moment.
#
# THE CLIENT TOOLS MUST MATCH THE SERVER
#
# pg_dump refuses to dump a server newer than itself. Debian bookworm, which the
# application image is built on, ships the PostgreSQL 15 client; the database is
# 16. Adding the upstream apt repository to the application image would work
# until the day somebody upgrades the server and the nightly backup starts
# failing for a reason nobody connects to the upgrade. Building FROM postgres:16
# makes the match true by construction: the client here is the same build as the
# server, always, because it is the same image.
#
# NODE, FOR THE PARTS THAT ARE NOT A SHELL PIPELINE
#
# Encryption with an authenticated cipher, a manifest, and a rehearsal that
# compares a restored database against what was recorded. Written in shell those
# are fragile; written in node they are ordinary. Node is copied in from the
# official image rather than installed, because both images are bookworm and the
# binary is all that is needed. No npm packages are installed here at all: the
# backup tooling deliberately depends on nothing but node's standard library and
# postgres's own client tools, so that it still runs on a day when the
# application will not.
#
# IT HOLDS DIFFERENT KEYS
#
# This container needs the owner connection, which can read every letter in the
# building, and the backup passphrase. The web server needs neither. Keeping them
# in a separate container means a flaw in a web page cannot reach either one.

FROM postgres:16

COPY --from=node:20-bookworm /usr/local/bin/node /usr/local/bin/node

WORKDIR /srv
COPY --chown=postgres:postgres . .
RUN chmod +x /srv/backup-entrypoint.sh \
 && mkdir -p /srv/backups \
 && chown postgres:postgres /srv/backups

# The postgres image's entrypoint exists to start a server. This one does not
# start anything; it takes backups of somebody else's.
ENTRYPOINT ["/srv/backup-entrypoint.sh"]
CMD ["schedule"]
USER postgres
